// Steer a one-way datagram stream to another box. `dnat(ip, port)` rewrites the
// destination (and fixes the IP and UDP checksums); `redirect(ifindex)` then
// forwards the packet straight out another interface (bpf_redirect, XDP_REDIRECT)
// instead of handing it to the local stack. redirect is terminal — it takes
// precedence over the verdict — and does not compose with redact/replace.
//
// Here: syslog from anything outside the trusted ranges is not delivered to the
// real collector; it is rewritten to a quarantine collector and sent out
// interface 5 (say, the veth into an analysis namespace). Trusted senders are
// untouched. The ifindex is a property of the host, so whatever deploys the
// program fills it in.
//
// For a request/reply protocol or TCP, use dnat alone and let the stack route:
// dnat is two-way (replies are translated back) — see honeypot.pfc.
filter {
  if (proto != 17) decide pass;          // UDP
  if (dport != 514) decide pass;         // syslog
  if (src in "10.0.0.0/8") decide pass;  // trusted senders reach the real collector
  if (src in "192.168.0.0/16") decide pass;

  dnat("10.99.0.2", 5514);               // the quarantine collector
  redirect(5);                            // out the analysis interface
  decide pass;
}
